var _markdown = "\n\n## What is Context? [#what-is-context]\n\nEvery middleware, guard, and handler receives the same object: the request context. It is a typed object that carries everything the current request needs — parsed input, session and tenant, application state, dependencies, and the knobs for shaping the response. Nothing useful is fetched from a global or ambient source; everything arrives through the context, which is how the framework keeps every handler explicit and testable.\n\nThe context is assembled once during request processing, after routing and before validation. Assembly follows the fixed order described in [Lifecycle](/docs/core-concepts/lifecycle): parse query and body, decode cookies and load the session, resolve the tenant, then run `state`, `decorate`, and `resolve`. After assembly the context is threaded through validation, guards, the handler, and response hooks unchanged.\n\n## Using Context in Handlers [#using-context-in-handlers]\n\n```ts title=\"using-context-in-handlers.ts\"\ndefineController('posts', (c) => ({\n  create: c.post('/', async (ctx) => {\n    // ctx.body     — typed request body\n    // ctx.params   — typed URL parameters\n    // ctx.query    — typed query string\n    // ctx.session  — the current session\n    // ctx.tenant   — the current tenant\n    // ctx.store    — per-request application state\n\n    const post = await Post.create({\n      ...ctx.body,\n      authorId: ctx.session.user.id,\n      tenantId: ctx.tenant.id,\n    })\n\n    return post\n  }),\n}))\n```\n\nThe same context is available in middleware and guards, so code that runs before the handler can read and set values that handlers depend on.\n\n## Typed Per-Route Context [#typed-per-route-context]\n\nContext types are inferred, not declared. Each property's type comes from a definition elsewhere in the app:\n\n| Property       | Type source                               |\n| -------------- | ----------------------------------------- |\n| `ctx.body`     | Validation schema in the route definition |\n| `ctx.params`   | URL parameter patterns                    |\n| `ctx.query`    | Query validation schema                   |\n| `ctx.headers`  | Header validation schema                  |\n| `ctx.cookies`  | Cookie validation schema                  |\n| `ctx.session`  | Auth configuration                        |\n| `ctx.tenant`   | Tenancy configuration                     |\n| `ctx.store`    | `state` declared in `defineApp`           |\n| decorated keys | `decorate` declared in `defineApp`        |\n| resolved keys  | `resolve` declared in `defineApp`         |\n\nBecause the route declares its own schemas, `params.id` is a `string`, `body` matches the body schema exactly, and invalid input never reaches the typed value the handler sees — validation runs first. See [Validation](/docs/http/validation) for what is checked, and [Type Inference](/docs/core-concepts/type-inference) for how the types are derived.\n\nConcretely, the route definition is what produces the handler's types:\n\n```ts title=\"typed-per-route-context.ts\"\nc.post('/', handler, {\n  body: { title: 'string', body: 'string?' },   // ctx.body: { title: string, body?: string }\n  query: { draft: 'string?' },                  // ctx.query: { draft?: string }\n  params: { id: 'string' },                     // ctx.params: { id: string }\n})\n```\n\nChange the schema and the handler re-types; the handler does not and cannot declare a conflicting body type.\n\n## A Walk Through Assembly [#a-walk-through-assembly]\n\nThe context is assembled in a fixed order during request processing, so every downstream stage sees a progressively richer object:\n\n1. **Parse** — query string and body are parsed (JSON, form, multipart) into `ctx.query` and raw body.\n2. **Decode and load** — cookies are decoded and the session is loaded into `ctx.session` from the configured store.\n3. **Resolve tenant** — tenant resolution runs from `src/config/tenancy.ts` (`subdomain`, `path`, `header`, or `fixed`) and lands on `ctx.tenant`.\n4. **Run app extensions** — `state` initializes the store, `decorate` attaches singletons, and `resolve` computes per-request values.\n\nOnly after assembly does validation run against the schemas, followed by guards and the handler. The ordering guarantees are documented on [Lifecycle](/docs/core-concepts/lifecycle); the key consequence for app code is that a guard's `beforeHandle` always sees the resolved session, tenant, and validated input.\n\n## Context in Middleware and Guards [#context-in-middleware-and-guards]\n\nMiddleware and guards read and write the same context the handler sees. Middleware typically fills the store before the handler:\n\n```ts title=\"context-in-middleware-and-guards.ts\"\ndefineMiddleware.on('onRequest', async (ctx, next) => {\n  ctx.store.requestId = ctx.headers['x-request-id'] ?? crypto.randomUUID()\n  return next()\n})\n```\n\nGuards read context to make authorization decisions, and can return an error through the same `error` member a handler would use:\n\n```ts title=\"context-in-middleware-and-guards-2.ts\"\ndefineController('admin', (c) => c.guard({\n  beforeHandle: ({ session, error }) => {\n    if (session.user?.role !== 'admin') return error('FORBIDDEN')\n  },\n}, (g) => ({\n  dashboard: g.get('/dashboard', dashboardHandler),\n})))\n```\n\nWhat a guard writes is visible to the handler; what a handler writes is visible to `onAfterHandle` and `onResponse` hooks.\n\n## Response Controls: set and error [#response-controls-set-and-error]\n\nTwo context members control what happens to the value a handler returns:\n\n```ts title=\"response-controls-set-and-error.ts\"\nhandler: async ({ params, query, body, headers, cookies, session, tenant, store, set, error }) => {\n  set.status = 201\n  set.headers['x-cache'] = 'miss'\n  return { id: params.id, ...body }\n}\n```\n\n* `set` carries status (defaults to 200), response headers, and cookie mutations. Mutating `set` after returning a value produces a fully formed response.\n* `error` returns a typed error response that maps into the shared taxonomy — `return error('NOT_FOUND', { message: 'no such post' })`. See [Error Handling](/docs/core-concepts/error-handling).\n\nReturning a plain object or array serializes to JSON; returning a web `Response` passes through unchanged.\n\n## Extending Context: state, decorate, resolve [#extending-context-state-decorate-resolve]\n\nThree mechanisms in `defineApp` shape what the context contains, and they differ in when and how values are produced:\n\n* **`state`** — declares typed fields on `ctx.store`. It is app-scoped state that lives on the per-request store.\n* **`decorate`** — attaches singletons that appear on the context unchanged and are created once at boot.\n* **`resolve`** — derives per-request values asynchronously during context assembly; it runs on every request and can read request data.\n\n```ts title=\"extending-context-state-decorate-resolve.ts\"\ndefineApp({\n  state: {\n    startTime: Date.now(),\n  },\n  decorate: {\n    client: createClient(),\n  },\n  resolve: async ({ headers, store }) => ({\n    tenant: await resolveTenant(headers),\n    requestStart: store.startTime,\n  }),\n})\n```\n\nHandlers then see the assembled result — `ctx.tenant`, `ctx.client`, `ctx.store.startTime` — all typechecked. `resolve` entries may be provided as an array of named resolvers, which is useful when derivations share intermediate work.\n\n> \\[!TIP]\n> Rules of thumb: `state` for typed request state, `decorate` for immutable singletons that exist once, and `resolve` for per-request values that depend on the inbound request (headers, path, cookies).\n\n## Reading Data Middleware Produces [#reading-data-middleware-produces]\n\nBecause middleware runs before the handler, it can enrich the context for the handler and for everything downstream:\n\n```ts title=\"reading-data-middleware-produces.ts\"\ndefineMiddleware('request-id', async (ctx, next) => {\n  ctx.store.requestId = ctx.headers['x-request-id'] ?? crypto.randomUUID()\n  return next()\n})\n```\n\nSession, tenant, and request ID are exactly this pattern internally: produced by pipeline stages, consumed by handlers, and typed through the assembled context.\n\n## Work After the Response [#work-after-the-response]\n\nThe context exposes `waitUntil` for post-response work. It keeps the connection alive only as long as the task needs and is safe on edge runtimes:\n\n```ts title=\"work-after-the-response.ts\"\nhandler: async ({ waitUntil }) => {\n  waitUntil(telemetry.flush())\n  return { ok: true }\n}\n```\n\nBoth event emission and queue dispatch use this shape internally — they acknowledge in-band and complete durably. See [Lifecycle](/docs/core-concepts/lifecycle).\n\n## What's Next [#whats-next]\n\n1. [Lifecycle](/docs/core-concepts/lifecycle) — where the context is assembled, step by step\n2. [Guards](/docs/http/guards) — reading the context to enforce authorization\n3. [Validation](/docs/http/validation) — the schemas that type `body`, `query`, `params`, `headers`, and `cookies`\n4. [Error Handling](/docs/core-concepts/error-handling) — the `error` member and response shaping\n5. [Sessions](/docs/auth/sessions) — how `ctx.session` is loaded and typed\n";
